# Alembic migrations — AIGate

Schema migrations for the tables defined in `src/aigate/models.py`.

## How it is wired

- `alembic/env.py` imports `Base` from `aigate.models`, so `--autogenerate`
  always diffs against the real ORM metadata.
- The database URL is **not** hard-coded in `alembic.ini`. It comes from the app
  settings, i.e. `AIGATE_DATABASE_URL` (default `sqlite+aiosqlite:///./aigate.db`),
  so migrations target the same database the app uses.
- Engines are created through the async API (`async_engine_from_config`), since
  every supported URL uses an async driver (aiosqlite / asyncpg).
- `render_as_batch` is enabled for SQLite, which cannot `ALTER TABLE` directly.

## Commands

```bash
# apply all migrations
uv run alembic upgrade head

# roll back everything
uv run alembic downgrade base

# new revision after editing models.py
uv run alembic revision --autogenerate -m "add foo column"

# is the DB in sync with models.py?  (CI-friendly: exits non-zero on drift)
uv run alembic check

# current revision / history
uv run alembic current
uv run alembic history
```

Point these at a scratch database instead of the local one:

```bash
AIGATE_DATABASE_URL="sqlite+aiosqlite:///./alembic_check.db" uv run alembic upgrade head
```

## Relationship to `create_all`

`aigate.db.init_db()` still calls `Base.metadata.create_all` when
`AIGATE_AUTO_CREATE_SCHEMA=true` (the default), so local dev and the test suite
keep working unchanged. Alembic is the migration path; set
`AIGATE_AUTO_CREATE_SCHEMA=false` where migrations own the schema, otherwise
`create_all` will quietly create any table the migrations have not applied yet.